papers to look into:
    - Aït-Kaci, "Warren's Abstract Machine: A Tutorial Reconstruction" (1991)
    - Demoen, "Dynamic attributes, their hProlog implementation" (2002)
    - Appleby et al., "Garbage Collection for Prolog Based on WAM" (1988)
    - Tarau, "The BinProlog Experience: Architecture and Implementation Choices"
    - Tarau & Dahl, "Logic Programming and Logic Grammars with First-Order Continuations"
    - Lindholm, "Unification and the Efficient Execution of Prolog"
    - Zhou, "Parameter Passing and Control Stack Management in Prolog Implementation Revisited" (1996)

non-determinism support?

known bugs (found via test/ulrich/ and test/iso_quad.pl):
    - builtin_atom_concat (src/builtins.c): stack-use-after-return once the
      concatenated atom gets large (MAX_NAME, 16k) ASAN crash
      triggered by ulrich/select_quad.pl's self-referential select/3 case,
      and independently by lib/quad.pl's own crash-recovery code before it
      was rewritten to stream line-by-line instead of accumulating one
      big atom (see count_partial/4, stream_copy_lines/2 in lib/quad.pl)
    - term.c make_var: MAX_VARS table exhaustion is a hard assertion
      failure (abort), hit by test/iso_quad.pl's
      number_codes tests
    - sub_atom/atom_concat split strings by byte offset, not UTF-8
      character offset, can produce invalid truncated multi-byte
      sequences (see test/iso_quad.pl's 'Pécs' test, breaks strict XML
      parsers reading the JUnit report)
    - no occurs-check, and no cycle detection either: self-referential
      unification (member(X,X), select(E,Xs,Xs), nth0(N,[[]|Es],Es)) either
      hangs forever or crashes the printer on the resulting cyclic term
